John Tooby and Leda Cosmides sum it up in their primer on evolutionary psychology: "Our modern skulls house a stone age mind." This gap is called evolutionary mismatch. According to this hypothesis, many of our biases are old survival reflexes:
• negativity bias: better to flee for nothing than to miss a predator;
• in-group bias: being driven out of the clan meant death;
• confirmation bias: convincing the tribe mattered more than being right;
• loss aversion: losing one's meagre reserves meant famine;
• present bias: when food was scarce, sugar and fat were eaten at once.
